    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22110v1</id>
    <title>Unfragmenting Nawatl NMT: Single-Variant Specialization for Masewalkopa</title>
    <updated>2026-10-15T15:51:41Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22110v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.22110v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>In minoritized (low-resource) language technologies, a common response to data sparsity is to aggregate text across multiple varieties and treat the combined data as a single corpus. However, mixed corpora of closely related indigenous variants can produce an artificial register that is accountable to no single speaker community, a structural failure we call variant fragmentation. This issue is acute for minoritized languages native to Mexico, such as Nawatl. Building on a 70,000-pair multi-variant foundation, we specialize an mT5-large checkpoint to Masewalkopa (nhn), the Nawatl variety spoken in Santa Ana Tlacotenco. We curate 2,665 parallel pairs, reserve all sentence-level data for evaluation, and incorporate 192 elder-transcribed target sentences via asymmetric backtranslation. A custom finite-state analyzer reduces unanalyzed surface forms from 82.0% to 19.9% and supplies functional syntax tags as plain-text prefixes, allowing the model to reuse existing subword embeddings rather than relying on frozen special tokens. The resulting LoRA-v9c-b model achieves 58.46 dev chrF++ and 58.20/57.90 directional test chrF++. Finally, CTranslate2 int8 quantization reduces CPU latency from 137.5 to 1.8-2.6 seconds per request, enabling cost-bounded Cloud Run deployment.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-10-15T15:51:41Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CL"/>
