    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22086v1</id>
    <title>Multi-SFT: Permissively-Licensed Multilingual Instruction-Following</title>
    <updated>2026-10-15T15:39:41Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22086v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.22086v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Supervised fine-tuning datasets remain overwhelmingly English-centric and commercially restricted, as their content is frequently generated by proprietary models whose terms of service forbid training competing systems. We introduce Multi-SFT, a commercially permissive collection of 502,818 instruction-response pairs across five European languages, built via translation and regeneration pipelines for tasks where translation is insufficient. Human evaluation yields inter-annotator agreement of at least 0.87 across all datasets and languages. Fine-tuning nine models from three different families (EuroLLM, OLMo 2, Qwen3; 1B-32B) and evaluating them on 13 benchmarks shows that Multi-SFT alone is competitive with larger augmented mixes and official instruction-tuned releases, with decisive advantages on safety tasks.</summary>
