    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22053v1</id>
    <title>Name-Bias in Large Language Models</title>
    <updated>2026-10-15T15:19:55Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22053v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.22053v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Names carry demographic indicators such as age, gender, and race, which makes them a subtle but strong target of bias in large language models (LLMs). In this work, we present an evaluation of name-based bias across 6 local large language models spanning multiple providers and parameter scales. Using a fill-in-the-blank task, we prompt these models to choose between pairs of first names drawn from U.S. Social Security Administration datasets and a race classification dataset by Rosenman et al.. Our experimental de- sign isolates age and gender effects through balanced name pairings and sentence templates that share a fixed syntactic structure. Across 300,000 local LLM responses, we find consistent and measurable name bias. Models prefer names that are less stereotypically male, older, popular, and white. Furthermore, we find that name-bias is highly sentence-dependent, but not more than associated stereotypes, and gender is the most significant bias, followed by age, name popularity, and race. Our results demonstrate that name-based bias remains pervasive even in local LLMs, highlighting the necessity for standardized evaluation methods and mitigation strategies.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-10-15T15:19:55Z</published>
